 ```
+**Where the hub is.** `build_html.py` copies the hub's `apps/design.css` and `apps/hub.js` into `build/` (so the hub flavours open from `build/`
+with the person's real tokens and the SDK) and its `icons/sprite.svg` into this repo's `icons/` beside `art/` (the pages `<use>` symbols from
+`../icons/sprite.svg`). It finds the hub through `HUB_REPO` (the hub checkout's root, from the environment) or a sibling folder named
+`house-hub` or `App Hub`, of this repo or of the main checkout when this is a worktree. Every `open()` passes `encoding="utf-8"`: a Windows
+rebuild used to turn `facts.json` into mojibake ("No steam train â€" Electric Trolley") in the exports. `verify.py` serves `build/` over
+http on 127.0.0.1 (a `file://` page cannot `<use>` an external sprite, and geolocation needs a secure context), takes its debugging
+screenshots without letting a slow machine fail the run, and ends with `step checks: all sections OK` or exits non-zero.
+In the hub flavours `<link rel="stylesheet" href="design.css">` sits in `<head>` right after the pre-paint bootstrap (batch 9, VIS-TELL-1), so
+the first frame is the person's theme, not a dark or unstyled one; `hub.js` stays where `__HUBJS__` is.
+
 Open `build/dollywood_terrain_reference.html` in any browser. Progress is kept in that browser's
 localStorage under the key `dollywood-build-progress-v2`; clearing site data or moving the file resets it.
 
